<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"/><meta name="description" content="The story of Tullio Campagnolo, Croce d’Aune and the quick-release mechanism that changed the racing bicycle."/><meta property="og:image" content="https://teob.dk/images/stories/campagnolo-quick-release/quick-release-detail.webp"/><title>When the cold created the quick release · The Evolution of Bikes</title><link rel="stylesheet" href={`${base}/styles/global.css`}/><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com"/><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin/><link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=DM+Sans:wght@400;500;600&family=Libre+Caslon+Display&display=swap" rel="stylesheet"/></head><body><EnglishHeader current="stories" danishPath="/historier/campagnolo-quick-release/"/><main><article class="quick-page">
<header class="quick-hero"><p class="section-label">Campagnolo · Croce d’Aune · 1927</p><h1>When the cold created<br/><em>quick release</em></h1><p>A frozen gear change on a mountain pass became the beginning of one of the racing bicycle’s most important mechanisms.</p></header><figure class="quick-pass"><img src={`${base}/images/stories/campagnolo-quick-release/campagnolo-croce-daune.webp`} alt="Illustration of a cyclist trying to loosen his rear wheel on a snowy mountain pass"/><figcaption>An illustration of the moment on Croce d’Aune when a troublesome wheel change prompted a new idea.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="quick-opening"><p class="section-label">11 November 1927</p><p>Tullio Campagnolo stood on Croce d’Aune with cold, numb fingers and a rear wheel he could not loosen.</p></section><section class="quick-story"><aside><strong>1,011 m</strong><span>Croce d’Aune<br/>above sea level</span></aside><div><p>During the Gran Premio della Vittoria, the 26-year-old Italian cyclist needed to change gear for the next part of the race. Racing bicycles of the period could have a sprocket on either side of the rear wheel. To select the other gear, the rider had to stop, loosen the wing nuts, reverse the wheel and tighten it again.</p><p>The weather was freezing. Campagnolo’s hands were too cold to grip the nuts. As his rivals continued, he was left with a problem that ought to have had a better solution.</p><blockquote>The wheel had to come loose quickly — without tools and without a fight with a frozen nut.</blockquote></div></section>
<section class="quick-facts">{facts.map(([n,l])=><div><strong>{n}</strong><span>{l}</span></div>)}</section><section class="quick-invention"><div><p class="section-label">From problem to patent</p><h2>A small cam with a large effect</h2></div><div><p>Campagnolo’s solution was a hollow axle with a rod — a skewer — through the hub. An adjusting nut sat at one end and a lever with an eccentric cam at the other.</p><p>Closing the lever made the cam pull the two ends together and clamp the wheel into the frame’s dropouts. Opening it allowed the wheel to be removed in seconds.</p><p>Campagnolo received his first patent for the mechanism in 1930. Three years later, he founded the company that bore his name.</p></div></section>
<figure class="quick-hub"><img src={`${base}/images/stories/campagnolo-quick-release/quick-release-detail.webp`} alt="Campagnolo quick release with lever, skewer, springs and adjusting nut" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>The quick-release mechanism removed from the hub: the lever and eccentric cam, skewer, conical springs and adjusting nut.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="quick-mechanism"><p class="section-label">How it works</p><div class="mechanism-steps"><div><span>01</span><h3>Open</h3><p>The lever opens and the cam releases the clamping force.</p></div><div><span>02</span><h3>Adjust</h3><p>The nut on the opposite side determines the correct tension.</p></div><div><span>03</span><h3>Close</h3><p>The lever closes with firm resistance and clamps the wheel securely in place.</p></div></div><p class="safety-note">A quick release is not a wing nut: the lever must close through the cam action, not merely be turned.</p></section>
<section class="quick-consequence"><div><p class="section-label">Beyond a faster wheel change</p><h2>The invention also changed gear shifting</h2></div><div><p>At first, the mechanism made it much quicker to reverse the rear wheel between two sprockets. Later, quick release became a major advantage for punctures and wheel changes in races.</p><p>The principle also became part of Campagnolo’s early gear systems. Cambio Corsa used one long lever to release the rear wheel and another arm to move the chain. Quick release was therefore both an invention in its own right and a step towards modern gears.</p></div></section><section class="quick-closing"><blockquote>A bad day on a mountain pass<br/>became a good day for <em>every cyclist.</em></blockquote></section>
<section class="period-sources"><p class="section-label">Sources and notes</p><div><p>The dates have been checked against histories of technology and cycling. The Croce d’Aune story is central to Campagnolo’s history, but accounts vary in some details concerning the race and date. The opening image is an illustration, not a contemporary documentary photograph.</p><ul><li><a href="https://www.dmg-lib.org/dmglib/handler?biogr=24804004">DMG-Lib: Tullio Campagnolo</a></li><li><a href="https://www.cyclingweekly.com/news/latest-news/icons-cycling-campagnolo-record-hub-284580">Cycling Weekly: Campagnolo Record hub</a></li></ul></div></section><nav class="era-bottom-nav" aria-label="Further navigation"><a href={`${base}/en/stories/`}>← All stories</a><a href={`${base}/en/stories/aldo-bini-maglia-nera-1948/`}>Next story →</a></nav></article></main><EnglishFooter/></body></html>
